# Introduction and goals

{% callout type="info" title="About this chapter" %}
Describe *why* the system exists: the essential functional requirements, the three to five most important quality goals and the people affected by the architecture. Keep this chapter short – details belong in chapter 10.
{% /callout %}

## Requirements overview

{% $system %} helps {% $organization %} plan and monitor its fleet of around 480 delivery vehicles. The system replaces a patchwork of spreadsheets and standalone tools.

The key use cases:

1. Plan the next day's routes and assign them to vehicles
2. Track vehicle positions and delivery status in real time
3. Schedule maintenance proactively and coordinate it with workshops
4. Analyze costs per vehicle and per route

## Quality goals

| Priority | Quality goal | Motivation |
|:---:|:---|:---|
| 1 | Availability | Dispatch works in shifts that start at 4 a.m. |
| 2 | Usability | Drivers use the app on the road and can spare little attention. |
| 3 | Modifiability | New telematics providers must be added without restructuring the system. |
| 4 | Data protection | Position data is personal data and subject to the GDPR. |

## Stakeholders

| Role | Expectations of the architecture |
|:---|:---|
| Head of Dispatch | Reliable daily planning, even when individual services fail |
| Drivers | A simple, fast app that also works offline |
| IT Operations | Standardized operation on the existing Kubernetes platform |
| Data Protection Officer | Traceable storage and deletion of position data |
| Development team | Clear interfaces and independently deployable building blocks |
